## Migration: `Thread.Abort` Timeout Pattern → .NET 8 Cooperative Cancellation

### Pattern Identified: **Timeout Enforcement**

Your code forcibly aborts a thread after a timeout. In .NET 8, `Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` (SYSLIB0006). The replacement is `CancellationToken` with `Task.WhenAny`.

---

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class TimedExecutor
{
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
        var token = cts.Token;

        var task = Task.Run(() => work(), token);

        try
        {
            // Task.WaitAsync throws OperationCanceledException on timeout
            return task.WaitAsync(token).GetAwaiter().GetResult();
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException($"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
        catch (AggregateException ex) when (ex.InnerExceptions.Count == 1)
        {
            throw new AggregateException(ex.InnerException!);
        }
    }
}
```

### If `work` is long-running and must actually stop (not just be abandoned)

Pass the token into the work delegate so it can cooperate:

```csharp
public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<CancellationToken, T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
{
    using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
    var token = cts.Token;

    var task = Task.Run(() => work(token), token);

    try
    {
        return task.WaitAsync(token).GetAwaiter().GetResult();
    }
    catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
    {
        throw new TimeoutException($"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
    }
}
```

Callers then check the token inside their work:

```csharp
executor.ExecuteWithTimeout(token =>
{
    foreach (var item in items)
    {
        token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested(); // cooperative checkpoint
        Process(item);
    }
    return result;
}, TimeSpan.FromSeconds(30));
```

---

### Key Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Guidance |
|---------|----------|
| **"Fire and forget" without cooperation** | The first overload *abandons* the task but doesn't stop it. The thread keeps running until `work()` finishes. Only use if the work is short or you accept resource leakage. |
| **Not disposing `CancellationTokenSource`** | Always wrap in `using`. Leaks timers otherwise. |
| **Expecting immediate stop** | Cancellation only works at checkpoints (`ThrowIfCancellationRequested`, async awaits). Long CPU-bound work without checkpoints won't stop. |
| **Blocking I/O won't cancel** | `Thread.Sleep`, `socket.Read`, etc. ignore the token. Use `token.WaitHandle.WaitOne(ms)` or async I/O with token overloads. |
| **`ThreadAbortException` catch is gone** | The old catch block with `ResetAbort` is deleted entirely — `OperationCanceledException` replaces it and does not auto-rethrow. |

---

### Removed APIs Summary

| Old (.NET Framework) | New (.NET 8) |
|---|---|
| `thread.Abort()` | `cts.Cancel()` / `CancelAfter(timeout)` |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Removed — not needed |
| Manual `Thread` + `Join(timeout)` | `Task.Run` + `Task.WaitAsync(token)` |

### Fully async alternative (preferred if callers are async)

```csharp
public async Task<T> ExecuteWithTimeoutAsync<T>(
    Func<CancellationToken, Task<T>> work, TimeSpan timeout)
{
    using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
    try
    {
        return await work(cts.Token).WaitAsync(cts.Token);
    }
    catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
    {
        throw new TimeoutException($"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
    }
}
```

This is the cleanest path for .NET 8 and avoids blocking thread pool threads entirely.
